IT Procurement Act","introducedDate":"2025-06-25","latestActionDate":"2026-07-21","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Advisory bodies","Competition and antitrust","Computers and information technology","Congressional oversight","Employment and training programs","Public contracts and procurement","Small business"],"sponsorBioguideId":"B001316","cosponsorCount":3,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4123","industries":[{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill mandates new IT and AI acquisition training for federal buyers and changes procurement thresholds, directly affecting how government purchases technology products and services."},{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Defense contractors participate heavily in federal procurement and would be affected by changes to acquisition thresholds, past-performance criteria, and small-business entry barriers."}],"plainSummary":"This bill updates federal government purchasing rules and training programs. It raises the dollar thresholds that determine when agencies can use simplified buying procedures, doubles the micro-purchase limit to $25,000 and increases the simplified acquisition threshold to $500,000. It also requires new training programs for federal acquisition staff on information technology and artificial intelligence, directs agencies to accept a wider range of past-project experience from bidders, and calls for steps to make it easier for small businesses to compete for government contracts.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People who run a small business.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S001230","score":0.03505955489339875},"overlapCount":4},"meaning":{"percentile":77.72863686621388,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8305262227557928}},{"billId":"119-hr-9961","bioguideId":"L000596","cycle":2026,"score":0.011332786364984873,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":16500,"donorShare":0.011332786364984873,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.011332786364984873}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8305262227557928},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-9961","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"9961","title":"To prohibit the use of campaign contributions or legal defense funds to pay settlements or penalties related to sexual assault, sexual abuse, and sexual harassment claims, and for other purposes.","introducedDate":"2026-07-27","latestActionDate":"2026-07-27","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on House Administration.","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000389","cosponsorCount":1,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/9961","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill affects how legal settlements and defense costs may be paid in sexual misconduct cases involving political figures."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would prohibit the use of campaign contributions or legal defense funds to pay settlements or penalties arising from sexual assault, sexual abuse, or sexual harassment claims. It would apply to federal candidates and officeholders, preventing them from using political funds to resolve these specific types of legal claims. The bill aims to ensure that such settlements are paid from personal funds rather than donor contributions.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"We cannot tell from the labels on this bill who it reaches. The subject is too broad to say.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"K000389","score":0.013834690132138347},"overlapCount":2},"meaning":{"percentile":52.503553940925606,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8305262227557928}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"L000596","cycle":2026,"score":0.005504496234421224,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":16500,"donorShare":0.011332786364984873,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.005504496234421224}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8305262227557928},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal 